\paragraph{Causal role of embedding training.}
The three-way ablation (trained/frozen/removed $\dt$-embedding; Table~\ref{tab:causal_ablation}) disambiguates two hypotheses: CIVO could arise from the architectural presence of a $\dt$-conditioning pathway, or specifically from learned $\dt$-specific representations shaped by gradient flow. Since frozen embeddings participate in the forward pass identically to trained ones yet do not trigger CIVO (NVE $0.40\times$ vs.\ $6.57\times$), the vulnerability lies in what the embedding \emph{learns}---near-antipodal representations (\S\ref{sec:cosine_evolution}) that amplify reward prediction gaps---rather than in conditioning architecture per se. For world-model RL systems using DreamerV3's RSSM, this suggests that auxiliary conditioning signals whose gradient flow touches the dynamics model require fidelity guarantees matching the model's prediction accuracy; without such guarantees, learned conditioning creates exploitable structure that imagination-based critics cannot self-correct.

